# The hero

The hero is the first screen, and it has one job: say what Loom is, and put the command that installs it within reach. Nothing else.

It opens with the mark and the wordmark at full size, then divides. On the left, the claim and the paragraph that unpacks it. On the right, the install line — one command, written for whichever package manager the reader actually has — and two links onward.

Those two links carry no rule under them and no colour of their own. They are ink until the cursor arrives, and then each takes the colour of the page it opens — the documentation's blue, the source's orange — which is the same ink the pager gives those pages at the foot of every page on the site.

The claim is a plain heading. It does not rotate a word, cycle a phrase or type itself out: a reader arriving on a page they have never seen should be able to read the first sentence without waiting for it to finish moving.

## The wordmark, at full size

The hero's mark is the pill and the wordmark side by side, and this is the only place either is drawn above its intrinsic size — the pill at 1.2, the wordmark at fifty-six pixels. The pair centres itself even though everything below it ranges left, because a mark this size reads as a masthead rather than as the first line of a column.

The wordmark carries a little padding on its right so the descender of its last letter does not sit hard against whatever follows.

=>

import { Array, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import type { Html, HtmlBuilder } from 'foldkit/html'
import { Copied, PickedRuntime, WentTo, type Message, type Model, type Runtime } from './model'
import { bunIcon, loomMark, npmIcon, pnpmIcon, terminalIcon } from './components'

const wordmark = (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div([h.Class('hero-mark')], [loomMark('page', 1.2), h.div([h.Class('hero-word')], ['Loom'])])

## The claim

The heading says what Loom is and what it is for, in one sentence: a literate programming framework that keeps meaning in a codebase from evaporating. The paragraph beneath it says how, in one more: the program is written like a book, its reasoning in prose and its code, the two as one source. The paragraph stops at forty-six characters a line. That is narrower than the site's usual prose measure, because the paragraph sits beside a column of its own, and a longer line would run under the install box.

=>

const claim = (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('hero-claim')],
    [
      h.h1([], ['Literate programming framework that keeps meaning in your codebase from evaporating']),
      h.p(
        [h.Class('hero-lead')],
        [
          'Write your program the way you write a book, with the reasoning in prose and the code — as one source.',
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

## One command, in the reader's own package manager

Loom installs four ways. Three package managers install the npm package, and a reader with no JavaScript tooling runs the install script with `curl`, which fetches the standalone executable. The hero shows one line rather than four. A reader picks their own from a row of tabs and the line beneath rewrites itself.

Each package manager's tab carries its real logo, vendored as inline markup rather than fetched as a file, so the mark is sharp at any size and the page makes no request for it. The `curl` tab carries a terminal prompt instead, the one tab no logo stands for. The chosen tab darkens to primary ink and draws a rule beneath itself; the others sit in muted ink with a transparent rule, so the row's baseline never shifts as the choice moves.

The command a tab shows and the command the button copies are the same string, read from the same row of the table. Writing them twice would be an invitation for one to drift while the other stays right. The install line is exported, because the documentation draws the very same widget wherever it tells a reader to install Loom, so the landing and the docs never offer two different commands. Its button is exported apart from it as `commandButton`: a prompt, the command, and a label that flashes `copied` once pressed. The documentation sets every one-line command it shows as one of these. The button flashes only when the model's copied id is its own, so two commands on one page never both flash.

=>

type Install = {
  readonly runtime: Runtime
  readonly label: string
  readonly command: string
  readonly logo: Html
}

const installs: ReadonlyArray<Install> = [
  { runtime: 'bun', label: 'bun', command: 'bun add -g @athrio/loom', logo: bunIcon() },
  { runtime: 'npm', label: 'npm', command: 'npm install -g @athrio/loom', logo: npmIcon() },
  { runtime: 'pnpm', label: 'pnpm', command: 'pnpm add -g @athrio/loom', logo: pnpmIcon() },
  {
    runtime: 'curl',
    label: 'curl',
    command: 'curl -fsSL https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh',
    logo: terminalIcon(),
  },
]

const commandFor = (runtime: Runtime): string =>
  pipe(
    Array.findFirst(installs, (install) => install.runtime === runtime),
    Option.map((install) => install.command),
    Option.getOrElse(() => ''),
  )

~

The command line is a button, not a code block, because its whole purpose is to be taken. Pressing it copies the line and the label at its end turns from `copy` to `copied` in the green that names `loom init`, then turns back a moment later.

=>

const runtimeTab = (install: Install, here: boolean, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.button(
    [
      h.Class(here ? 'rt-tab here' : 'rt-tab'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.OnClick(PickedRuntime({ runtime: install.runtime })),
    ],
    [install.logo, install.label],
  )

export const commandButton = (
  id: string,
  command: string,
  copied: string,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const taken = copied === id
  return h.button(
    [
      h.Class('rt-command'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.Title('Copy the command'),
      h.OnClick(Copied({ id, text: command })),
    ],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('prompt')], ['$']),
      h.span([h.Class('line')], [command]),
      h.span([h.Class(taken ? 'took done' : 'took')], [taken ? 'copied' : 'copy']),
    ],
  )
}

export const installLine = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('hero-install')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('rt-tabs')],
        Array.map(installs, (install) => runtimeTab(install, install.runtime === model.runtime, h)),
      ),
      commandButton('install', commandFor(model.runtime), model.copied, h),
    ],
  )

## The way onward

Two links close the hero: one into the documentation, one into the source. Each carries a rule beneath it rather than an underline, which keeps the descenders clear of the line. The documentation link switches the page on the spot, as the nav's links do. The source link leaves the move to the browser, since the client holds no book to draw the source page with.

=>

const onward = (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('hero-onward')],
    [
      h.a(
        [
          h.Class('hero-link'),
          h.Href('/docs'),
          h.Style({ '--hero-ink': '#1D4FBF' }),
          h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: 'docs' })),
        ],
        ['Read the docs →'],
      ),
      h.a(
        [
          h.Class('hero-link'),
          h.Href('/source'),
          h.Style({ '--hero-ink': '#C2410C' }),
        ],
        ['Browse the source'],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The hero, assembled

The wordmark sits above two columns that become one below seven hundred and sixty-five pixels, where a line of prose and an install box can no longer stand side by side.

=>

export const hero = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('section section-hero'), h.Id('loom-top')],
    [
      wordmark(h),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('hero-columns')],
        [claim(h), h.div([h.Class('hero-right')], [installLine(model, h), onward(h)])],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The module {Tangle}[hero.ts]

The module is the wordmark, the claim, the install line and the links, then the hero that holds them.

